      "id": 228371,
      "name": "aramis",
      "username": "aramis",
      "avatar_template": "/user_avatar/forums.greaterthangames.com/aramis/{size}/1871_2.png",
      "created_at": "2019-08-10T01:06:50.000Z",
      "cooked": "<aside class=\"quote group-playtesters\" data-username=\"Rabit\">\n<div class=\"title\">\n<div class=\"quote-controls\"></div>\n<img loading=\"lazy\" alt=\"\" width=\"24\" height=\"24\" src=\"https://forums.greaterthangames.com/user_avatar/forums.greaterthangames.com/rabit/48/5774_2.png\" class=\"avatar\"> Rabit:</div>\n<blockquote>\n<p>SCRPG is a very flexible and versatile system. If we start creating rules around how it needs to be consistent for things like this, I think we’ll lose one of its greatest strengths. Having examples of how to do this would be great, though, so folks understand what’s possible and how it might work, but I personally don’t think an official system to manage it would be good.</p>\n<p>Doesn’t stop us from discussing how it might work, though. <img src=\"https://forums.greaterthangames.com/images/emoji/apple/smiley.png?v=12\" title=\":smiley:\" class=\"emoji\" alt=\":smiley:\" loading=\"lazy\" width=\"20\" height=\"20\"></p>\n</blockquote>\n</aside>\n<p>The point of RPG rules at all is consistency, so that decisions can be made on an informed basis by players.</p>\n<p>Flexibility at the cost of clarity only works for a subset of players and GMs.</p>\n<p>Excess flexibility means a harder learning curve for players and GMs, as well</p>",
